.TH LSEEK 2
.SH NAME
lseek, tell \- move read/write pointer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B "long lseek(fildes, offset, whence)"
.B "long offset;"
.sp
.B "long tell(fildes)"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The file descriptor
.I fildes
refers to a file open for reading or writing.
The
read (resp. write) pointer for the file is set as follows:
.RS
.PP
If
.I whence
is 0, the pointer is set to
.I offset
bytes.
.PP
If
.I whence
is 1, the pointer is set to its current location plus
.IR offset .
.PP
If
.I whence
is 2, the pointer is set to the end of the file plus
.IR offset .
.RE
.PP
The returned value is the resulting pointer location.
.PP
The obsolete function
\fBtell(\fIfildes\fB)\fR
is identical to
\fBlseek(\fIfildes\fB, 0L, 1).\fR
.PP
Seeking far beyond the end of a file, then writing, creates a gap or `hole',
which occupies no physical space and reads as zeros.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
open(2), creat(2), fseek(3)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
\-1 is returned for an undefined file descriptor, seek on a pipe, or seek to
a position before the beginning of file.
.SH BUGS
.B lseek
is a no-op on character special files.
.SH ASSEMBLER
(lseek = 19.)
.TP
PDP-11:
.nf
R0 = fildes
sys lseek; offset1; offset2; whence
.fi
.sp
.I offset1
and
.I offset2
are the high and low words of
.IR offset ;
R0 and
R1 contain the pointer upon return.
.TP
8086:
.nf
BX = 19
AX = file descriptor
DX = offset 1
CX = offset 2
SI = whence
int 0Xf1
.fi
.sp
.I offset1
and
.I offset2
are the high and low words of
.IR offset ;
AX
and DX contain the pointer upon return.
.sp
